    <h2>Which calculator to use</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shopping for a home loan:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/mortgage.html">mortgage calculator</a> gives the full monthly housing payment, including taxes, insurance, HOA dues, and PMI.</li>
      <li><strong>Considering a new rate on an existing mortgage:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/refinance.html">refinance calculator</a> finds the break-even month and compares total interest on both paths.</li>
      <li><strong>Car, personal, or student loans:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/loan.html">loan and amortization calculator</a> handles any fixed-rate installment loan and shows what extra payments save.</li>
      <li><strong>Deciding where extra money should go:</strong> the <a href="../../calculators/pay-off-mortgage-or-invest.html">pay off mortgage or invest calculator</a> finds the investment return you would need to beat prepaying.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The 3 numbers that matter on any loan</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The monthly payment</strong> tells you whether you can afford it. It's the number lenders lead with, and the easiest to make look small by stretching the term.</li>
      <li><strong>Total interest</strong> tells you what the loan really costs. A $320,000 mortgage at 6.5% costs about $408,000 in interest over 30 years, but about $182,000 over 15.</li>
      <li><strong>The APR</strong> lets you compare lenders, because it folds certain fees into the rate. 2 loans with the same rate can have different APRs if one charges more up front.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How amortization works against you early</h2>
    <p>Fixed-rate loans charge interest on the remaining balance each month. Early on the balance is high, so most of each payment is interest; on a 30-year mortgage at 6.5%, about 86% of the 1st payment goes to interest. This has 2 practical consequences. Extra principal paid early saves the most interest, because it removes balance that would otherwise be charged for decades. And refinancing into a new 30-year term restarts that interest-heavy schedule, which is why a lower payment doesn't always mean a cheaper loan.</p>

    <h2>Before you sign</h2>
    <p>For mortgages, U.S. lenders must give you a standard Loan Estimate within 3 business days of applying. Because every lender uses the same form, it's the cleanest way to compare offers line by line. Ask whether the loan has a prepayment penalty, how extra payments are applied, and whether the quoted rate includes discount points, which raise your upfront cost in exchange for a lower rate.</p>
